[Music]  i'm ashley thomas a cognitive scientist  at the mcgovern institute at mit i use  puppets to study baby minds  and how babies make sense of the world  around them  people used to think that babies didn't  understand much at all about the world  but now we know that's wrong babies  understand a lot  even though babies can't talk their  behavior provides clues about what  they're thinking  babies look at people and things they  like and they focus their attention  where they expect things to happen  so to figure out what a baby is thinking  i measure what they look at  here's what i mean  this is baby ursula she is watching  sarah share food with a puppet  now ursula sees a different person laura  passing a ball with the puppet what  happens if the puppet starts crying who  will comfort the sad puppet sarah or  laura  ursula can't tell us what she is  thinking but she can show us  when ursula looks at sarah first it  tells me that she expects sarah to  comfort the puppet  these types of experiments show us that  babies do understand a lot about the  world around them including complex  social relationships  [Music]

